    "knowledge_point_id": "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$",
    "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
